<!--
    Avro parallel parsing: reading a single Avro object container file through the `file()` table
    function now splits it into segments on block (sync) boundaries and decodes them on several
    threads. Before this change the Avro reader had no `FileSegmentationEngine`, so
    `input_format_parallel_parsing` was a no-op for it and a large file was decoded on one thread
    regardless of `max_threads`.

    The file is written with the default sync interval, so 20M rows produce many blocks to segment
    on; the queries do only light aggregation, so the time is dominated by Avro decoding, which is
    what parallelizes.

    The first query turns parallel parsing on, the second is the control with it off. On this change
    the first query parses on several threads and the second on one, so the first should be several
    times faster; the gap guards that Avro reads keep parsing in parallel. The control also stays
    stable against the reference (master) binary, where parallel parsing was a no-op for Avro, so a
    cross-version comparison attributes the first query's speedup to parallelism rather than to some
    unrelated change in the read path.

    The read goes through `file()` rather than a `File` engine table on purpose: only the table
    function path parses in parallel.
-->
<test>
    <settings>
        <max_threads>16</max_threads>
    </settings>

    <fill_query><![CDATA[
        INSERT INTO FUNCTION file('test_avro_parallel_parsing.avro', Avro, 's String, n UInt64')
        SELECT concat('row_', toString(number)), number
        FROM numbers(20000000)
        SETTINGS engine_file_truncate_on_insert = 1
    ]]></fill_query>

    <query>SELECT sum(length(s)), sum(n) FROM file('test_avro_parallel_parsing.avro', Avro, 's String, n UInt64') SETTINGS input_format_parallel_parsing = 1 FORMAT Null</query>
    <query>SELECT sum(length(s)), sum(n) FROM file('test_avro_parallel_parsing.avro', Avro, 's String, n UInt64') SETTINGS input_format_parallel_parsing = 0 FORMAT Null</query>
</test>
